{"kind":"report","text":"PROCESS RETROSPECTIVE, measured over the whole log: 2026-08-06 to 2026-08-23, snapshot depth 8938, main d91fb8b6. Arithmetic first.\n\nWHAT I RE-DERIVED MYSELF before filing, because a retrospective that cites numbers it did not check is the thing it is complaining about: 196 merges to main by git log --merges --first-parent. 102 ineffective events of 8938, 1.1 percent, by joining projection.decisions to statements and acts. The reason grouping reproduces: report has no promise 23, dangling promise has no request 14, ratify target is unknown 14, actor may not supersede target 11, supersede target is unknown 10, request state requires body.conditions 7. 126 artifacts at dot or comma-joined paths, zero of them live. The code guards are where the review says: cmd/gs/succession.go:233 refuses publishing such a path, internal/workroom/fold.go:547 rules the artifact ineffective.\n\nTHE HEADLINE NUMBER. 456 of 1327 requests, 34 percent, were superseded, 449 of them by their own author. Roughly 266 of those were pure re-anchoring - same conditions, same addressee, same governing decision, moved only because a merge staled the basis. 175 promised commitments were cancelled unreported as collateral, every one of them with a live promise and no report. At two to four events per cycle that is on the order of 1000 to 1800 of 8938 events spent restating asks that had not changed. And it bought nothing: 196 merges landed while 73 percent of commitments were stale, because the merge gate reads only the approval.\n\nTHE SECOND NUMBER. 972 of 1327 requests, 73 percent, are review requests. Promise-to-report median is 8 minutes and 59 percent of reports follow their promise within 10 minutes. The promise leg exists to show work in flight and in the median case there is no flight to show.\n\nTHE ONE FINDING I WOULD PUT ABOVE THE REST, because it is evidence rather than opinion. Filing-shape refusals - 46 of them - ran from 08-07 to 08-12 and then stopped dead. Zero since. The prose in the work-loop section existed the whole time; what ended them was moving the rule into a write-boundary guard. Cross-actor supersession refusals, by contrast, are still occurring in the final week at one or two a week, and their remedy still lives only in AGENTS.md step 5. Code guards work where prose warnings did not, and this log contains the controlled comparison.\n\nRECOMMENDATIONS, INCLUDING THE REQUIRED REMOVAL.\n\nREMOVE: stop replacing requests for ordinary staleness. Rule 11 already says a promise or report is not replaced for ordinary staleness because exact-head review and gs merge record it. Extend that to requests: an ordinary-stale request whose conditions, addressee and governing decision are unchanged stays live as filed. This deletes ceremony rather than adding it, and removes the mechanism that cancelled 175 promised commitments. Testable: staleness-citing request supersessions per week should fall by an order of magnitude with no change in merge rate.\n\nREMOVE: the review promise. SKILL.md already calls a promise optional; AGENTS.md step 4 nonetheless prescribes one. Let the reviewer report the verdict directly against the review request, promising only when the review will genuinely be in flight. Nothing downstream depends on it - the merge gate reads the ratified approval alone.\n\nCODE GUARD, not more prose: resolve every citation at the write boundary. SKILL.md warns twice and the mode still produced 24 unknown-target refusals, and it documents that a citation resolving to nothing is admitted in silence, so those 24 are the visible tail of something unmeasured.\n\nMOVE, not more prose: put the cross-actor supersession guidance into the refusal message itself - name the target author and the co-sign path - rather than in a document the actor has already read.\n\nDROP: the path-hygiene and migration paragraphs in AGENTS.md step 5. Both rules are enforced in code at the two lines above, no bad-path artifact has been filed since 08-10, and the migration has zero live targets. Roughly 250 words warning against what the fold already refuses.\n\nONE CORRECTION TO THE MEASUREMENT I WAS GIVEN. It reports 9 paths carrying more than one live artifact as a residual defect. I confirmed 9 - Makefile, README.md, SKILL.md, cmd/gs, docs, internal/app, internal/apphost, internal/workroom, ui - but most of those extras are candidates on unmerged heads, and candidates do not collide with the merge preflight; only shipped live artifacts do. The figure overstates the defect and should not be filed as one without separating candidates from shipped.\n\nATTRIBUTION. Every recurring mode here is process-caused. The refile treadmill is what the rules instructed. The empty promises are what step 4 prescribed. The citation failures happened in a gap the write boundary deliberately left open. No actor is named, and none should be.\n\nThe two REMOVE items and the DROP touch AGENTS.md and SKILL.md, so they are hugh to ratify. I will file the proposal separately rather than bundle it here.","body":{"commit":"d91fb8b633d78b224c388eb96098825d9fd42c4b"}}